network-manager-gpclient (1.4.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Report the DNS servers and search domains the gateway pushes to
    NetworkManager in Ip4Config (closes #15). They used to be applied by
    vpnc-script alone, straight to systemd-resolved, so NetworkManager knew
    the tunnel only as an assumed device without DNS and wiped the VPN
    resolver on its next DNS recalculation (an IPv6 router advertisement on
    the physical interface was enough) while the tunnel stayed up. The
    connect.d hook now records INTERNAL_IP4_DNS / CISCO_DEF_DOMAIN /
    CISCO_SPLIT_DNS in /run/nm-gpclient/dns-state for the service to read
  * vpn.data dns still overrides the pushed servers; dns-domains are added to
    the pushed search domains

 -- WMP <example@example.org>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

network-manager-gpclient (1.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the connection never leaving "activating" when two other tun-based
    VPNs are already up (closes #13): tunnel detection no longer polls a
    fixed gpd0/tun0/tun1 list, which could not see our own tunnel once it
    landed on tun2 or higher, so NetworkManager's vpn.timeout tore the
    connection down. The candidates are read from /sys/class/net instead,
    and any number of foreign tunnels may be active
  * Attribute the tunnel to gpclient by the file descriptors it holds
    (iff: in /proc/PID/fdinfo), so another VPN activated during
    authentication is not adopted as ours

 -- WMP <example@example.org>  Wed, 26 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200

network-manager-gpclient (1.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix portal addresses: --gateway is no longer forced to the server address,
    which made gpclient abort with "Cannot find gateway specified" for every
    real portal (closes #7). The gateway prompt is answered by the service:
    vpn.data preferred-gateway if it matches, otherwise the portal's first
    proposal, with a fallback when a configured gateway is gone
  * Cache the portal's gateway list in the profile (vpn.data gateway-list)
    after a successful connection and offer it in the connection editors
  * New connection options: vpn.data as-gateway, vpn.data preferred-gateway
  * Fix the authentication browser closing after ~8 s: the new
    /usr/libexec/gpclient/browser-wrapper waits for gpauth to finish instead
    of guessing from tunnel interfaces (GP_AUTH_TIMEOUT overrides the 300 s
    safety timeout); edge-wrapper stays as a compatibility shim
  * Support any browser (edge, firefox, chrome, chromium, default) and import
    the user's session environment, so browsers other than Edge actually open
    a window on Wayland (closes #7)
  * Auth dialog: answer SAML connections with an empty secret set instead of
    an error, so opening the connection editor no longer waits out
    NetworkManager's 25 s secrets timeout (closes #8)
  * Expose Preferred gateway, Address is a gateway, Username and
    Authentication in the GNOME and Plasma connection editors
  * Work around portals that need legacy TLS renegotiation (closes #2): retry
    once with gpclient's --fix-openssl when it reports
    "unsafe legacy renegotiation disabled", then store fix-openssl=true in the
    profile so the failed first attempt does not repeat. New connection option
    vpn.data fix-openssl=auto|true|false, exposed in the editors
  * Emit the STOPPED state after a failure, so NetworkManager reports the error
    immediately instead of waiting out its connect timeout

 -- WMP <example@example.org>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 20:00:00 +0200

network-manager-gpclient (1.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Support portals with standard login / RSA token challenges (closes #6):
    run gpclient under a PTY, detect its interactive prompts and answer
    them from stored connection secrets or interactively via the
    NetworkManager SecretsRequired/NewSecrets flow
  * Add GNOME/nm-applet auth dialog (/usr/libexec/nm-gpclient-auth-dialog)
  * New connection options: vpn.data username, vpn.data auth-mode=credentials,
    vpn.secrets password
  * Add unit tests for prompt detection (make test-unit)

